

{"id":"f4dc30da-fda3-4e9a-bac9-9878554d677c","object":"chat.completion","created":1786438430,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_015\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_01\",\"depart_slot\":5,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_03\",\"depart_slot\":4,\"charge_blocks\":1},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_05\",\"depart_slot\":19,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_04\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_10\",\"depart_slot\":9,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_05\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_04\",\"depart_slot\":23,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_06\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_06\",\"depart_slot\":21,\"charge_blocks\":0}]}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task to produce a repair. The baseline has decisions for MISSION_01 to MISSION_06, with specific UAV assignments, paths, depart slots, charge_blocks 0, held false. We need to produce a JSON object with record_id \"CA12_CORE_015\" and a decisions array for the six missions. We must consider the intervention overrides and the fixed UAV assignment and mission order (the assignment_order_contract is FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER). So each mission is assigned to a specific UAV as given in the mission definitions: MISSION_01 -> UAV_03, MISSION_02 -> UAV_02, MISSION_03 -> UAV_03, MISSION_04 -> UAV_02, MISSION_05 -> UAV_03, MISSION_06 -> UAV_01. That is fixed.\n\nThe deadhead contract is FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH, meaning incoming deadhead paths are fixed and given in the mission? For each mission, incoming_deadhead is null. So no deadhead for any mission? Actually, the baseline has incoming_deadhead null for all missions, so no deadhead flight needed. The norm says \"incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". Since incoming deadhead is null, we just start with the UAV at origin vertiport? But note the fleet initial locations: all UAVs start at VP_06 (start_vertiport: \"VP_06\"). So for missions that originate at VP_06, no repositioning needed. For missions that originate elsewhere (MISSION_03 and MISSION_04 originate at VP_01, MISSION_05 at VP_07), we need to consider how the UAV gets to that origin. The incoming deadhead is null, but those missions are after previous missions that end at certain vertiports. So the UAV must already be at the origin vertiport after the previous mission's turnaround and charge. No deadhead needed because the previous mission's destination is the next mission's origin. Indeed, for UAV_03: MISSION_01 goes from VP_06 to VP_01; then MISSION_03 from VP_01 to VP_07; then MISSION_05 from VP_07 to VP_05. So it's a chain. For UAV_02: MISSION_02 from VP_06 to VP_01; then MISSION_04 from VP_01 to VP_03. So no deadhead required. For UAV_01: MISSION_06 from VP_06 to VP_02, only mission, starts at VP_06.\n\nSo the repair might need to adjust depart slots and paths due to interventions: closed corridors, capacity overrides, etc. Also initial_soc_override for UAV_02: initial_soc_kwh 1.710895 instead of baseline 2.760895. That's lower. Need to ensure energy feasibility.\n\nLet's list all missions with UAV assignment:\n\nUAV_03 (fixed_wing_vtol, capacity 22 kWh, reserve 2.5, charge per block 0.95, empty energy 0.145 kWh/km, payload energy 0.012 per kg per km, wind multiplier 0.065, max wind level 2, travel time coeff 0.86, compatible layers L120_220 and L220_320; initial SOC 5.006967, start VP_06):\n\n- MISSION_01: origin VP_06, dest VP_01, release 4, due 6, payload 0.5 kg. Path options: PATH_01 (COR_06, distance 1.248 km, travel slots 1, energy 0.200697, wind 1, max wind 1). So PATH_01 is only option. Baseline: depart 4.\n\n- MISSION_03: origin VP_01, dest VP_07, release 7, due 24, payload 0.5 kg. Path options: PATH_02 (COR_11, travel slots 2, energy 0.167248, wind 1), PATH_05 (COR_10, travel slots 2, energy 0.167248, wind 1), PATH_09 (COR_09, travel slots 14, energy 1.337981, wind 1). Note COR_11 and COR_10 have closures? Let's see intervention closed_corridor_slots: COR_11 closed from slot 7 to 46 (all slots from 7 to 46, covering the whole horizon 44). COR_10 closed from slot 7 to 18. So PATH_02 (uses COR_11) cannot be used if any slot of its travel falls within closed slots. PATH_02 travel slots: 2 slots, depart + travel slots. If depart slot >= 7, then slots 7-? would be closed. If depart slot is 5 or 6, then travel slots 5-6 maybe okay? But release is 7, so depart slot cannot be before 7. So PATH_02 depart would be >=7, thus slots 7-8 are closed (COR_11 closed 7-46). So PATH_02 infeasible. PATH_05 uses COR_10, closed from slot 7 to 18. If depart slot >=7 and travel slots 2, then slots 7-8 if depart 7, or 8-9 if depart 8, etc. Up to slot 18, closed. So if depart slot >=7 and <=17, the flight would occupy a slot within 7-18 where COR_10 closed. If depart slot 18, travel slots 18-19? Actually depart slot is start of travel? Typically travel slots are duration, if depart slot = d, travel occupies slots d, d+1, ..., d+travel_slots-1. For PATH_05 travel_slots=2. If depart 18, slots 18-19 are used. COR_10 closed 7-18, so slot 18 is closed, so depart 18 still closed. If depart 19, slots 19-20, that's open. So depart slot >=19 would avoid closure. But due slot is 24, so depart 19 + travel 2 = arrival slot 21? arrival slot = depart + travel_slots. Then turnaround 1 slot, post-service charge maybe. But reserve check, etc. So PATH_05 feasible if depart >=19. PATH_09 uses COR_09, not closed, travel slots 14, energy 1.337981, wind 1. That's long. Baseline had PATH_02 depart 7, but PATH_02 now infeasible due to COR_11 closure.\n\n- MISSION_05: origin VP_07, dest VP_05, release 23, due 27, payload 0.5 kg. Path options: PATH_04 (COR_01, travel slots 1, energy 1.337981, wind 1). Only one option.\n\nUAV_02 (multirotor, capacity 16, reserve 1.5, charge 1.2 per block, empty energy 0.1, payload energy 0.026, wind multiplier 0.11, max wind 1, travel time coeff 1, compatible L120_220 and L220_320; initial SOC overridden to 1.710895, start VP_06):\n\n- MISSION_02: origin VP_06, dest VP_01, release 4, due 10, payload 0.5 kg. Path options: PATH_03 (COR_06, travel slots 2, energy 0.156537, wind 1), PATH_07 (COR_05, travel slots 8, energy 1.356, wind 0). Baseline used PATH_03 depart 4.\n\n- MISSION_04: origin VP_01, dest VP_03, release 8, due 22, payload 0.5 kg. Path options: PATH_10 (COR_08, travel slots 1, energy 0.104358, wind 1). So only one path.\n\nUAV_01 (multirotor, capacity 16, reserve 1.5, charge 1.2 per block, empty 0.1, payload 0.026, wind multiplier 0.11, max wind 1, travel coeff 1, initial SOC 7.5, start VP_06):\n\n- MISSION_06: origin VP_06, dest VP_02, release 21, due 28, payload 0.5 kg. Path options: PATH_06 (COR_02, travel slots 2, energy 0.12543, wind 1), PATH_08 (COR_04 then COR_07, travel slots 2, energy 0.147827, wind 1 on first segment, 0 on second, but max wind level 1 for the path? max_wind_level 1, okay). Baseline used PATH_06 depart 21.\n\nNow, the baseline may be infeasible due to closures and SOC. The repair task says: \"Return one complete repair covering every mission exactly once. Respect fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and all intervention overrides. Optimize the stated task lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\"\n\nWe need to produce a decision for each mission: mission_id, uav_id, held (false/true), path_id (if held false, must choose a path), depart_slot (if held false, an integer within horizon? slot_min is 5, but depart slots can be from release_slot to horizon? horizon_slots 44), charge_blocks (integer 0..max_charge_blocks 3). If held true, path_id null, depart_slot null, charge_blocks 0.\n\nWe need to check feasibility: for each UAV, chain missions in sequence order (sequence_index gives order per UAV). For UAV_03: sequence_index 0,1,2 -> MISSION_01, MISSION_03, MISSION_05. For UAV_02: sequence_index 0,1 -> MISSION_02, MISSION_04. For UAV_01: sequence_index 0 -> MISSION_06.\n\nWe must respect release/due windows. For each mission, depart_slot must be >= release_slot, and completion (arrival at destination) must be <= due_slot? Actually, due_slot is when the mission must be completed? In previous tasks, due is the slot by which the mission must be completed (arrival at destination). Release is earliest depart. So arrival_slot = depart_slot + travel_slots must be <= due_slot. If arrive > due, held becomes necessary? Or maybe you can only schedule within window; if impossible, you must hold? But baseline doesn't hold. The optimization objective includes minimizing holds, and urgent holds first. So we may hold a mission if unable to schedule within window? But the instance says \"assignment_order_contract: FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\". Does that mean we cannot skip missions? Possibly we must schedule all missions; if a mission cannot be scheduled within its window, we must hold it. But the objective penalizes holds.\n\nWe need to check if the baseline is feasible after interventions. Let's examine each.\n\nFirst, SOD overrides: initial_soc_override for UAV_02 is 1.710895 kWh. Check energy for MISSION_02:\n\nUAV_02 initial SOC: 1.710895. MISSION_02: PATH_03 energy 0.156537. Reserve 1.5. Total needed: energy + reserve = 1.656537. So 1.710895 - 1.656537 = 0.054358 remaining after service + reserve check? But order: incoming deadhead (null) -> service flight -> reserve check. So after service flight, SOC = initial - service_energy. Then reserve check: SOC must be >= reserve. After that, we have destination turnaround (1 slot), then post-service charge. At destination VP_01, we can charge up to 3 blocks. So after turnaround, we can charge. Battery capacity is 16. So we need to ensure SOC after service >= reserve. 1.710895 - 0.156537 = 1.554358, which is > 1.5, so reserve ok. Tight but ok. Then at VP_01, we can charge for MISSION_04 which departs from VP_01 after MISSION_02's turnaround and charge. We need to ensure enough energy for MISSION_04 and its reserve.\n\nBut before that, baseline depart slot for MISSION_02 was 4. Release is 4, travel slots 2, arrival slot = 6? depart 4, travel 2, so arrival 6. Due 10, fine. PATH_03 uses COR_06. Check corridor_capacity_override: COR_06 capacity overridden to 1 at slot 4. So only one UAV can use COR_06 at slot 4. Baseline had UAV_03 using PATH_01 on COR_06 depart 4? MISSION_01: PATH_01 uses COR_06, depart 4, travel slots 1, so uses slot 4. So UAV_03 and UAV_02 both try to use COR_06 at slot 4? But corridor capacity COR_06 default 2, but override for slot 4 is 1. So only one can depart at slot 4 using COR_06. So conflict. We must stagger departures to avoid exceeding capacity 1 at slot 4. So one of them must shift. Also UAV_02's MISSION_02 could use PATH_07 (COR_05) which has no closure or capacity issues, but travel 8 slots, energy 1.356. With initial SOC 1.710895 - 1.356 = 0.354895, reserve 1.5? That's less than reserve, so infeasible. So PATH_07 not possible due to low SOC. So MISSION_02 must use PATH_03. Thus UAV_02 must use COR_06. But then UAV_03 MISSION_01 also uses COR_06. They can't both at slot 4. So we need to shift one. UAV_03's MISSION_01 due 6, travel 1, depart 4 or 5. If depart 5, arrival 6, still due 6, okay. Release 4. So shift MISSION_01 to depart 5 avoids slot 4 conflict? But check corridor capacity at slot 5: COR_06 capacity default 2, no override at slot 5? Override only at slot 4. So slot 5 is fine. So MISSION_01 could depart at 5. Or MISSION_02 could depart at 5? But MISSION_02 release 4, travel 2, depart 5 arrival 7, due 10, fine. But we must check if UAV_02 can move its depart to 5. That would avoid slot 4. However, initial SOC, can we wait? No energy penalty for waiting. So we can shift either. But also need to consider subsequent missions and energy.\n\nNow MISSION_03 for UAV_03: origin VP_01, release 7, due 24. Baseline depart 7 with PATH_02 (COR_11) which is closed from 7. So infeasible. So we need a different path. PATH_05 (COR_10) closed 7-18. If we depart after 18, say 19, travel 2, arrival 21, due 24, ok. PATH_09 (COR_09) travel 14, if depart 7, arrival 21, due 24 ok, but long travel, high energy 1.337981. So we need to check energy for UAV_03 chain.\n\nUAV_03 initial SOC 5.006967. MISSION_01: PATTH_01 energy 0.200697. So after MISSION_01, SOC = 5.006967 - 0.200697 = 4.80627. Reserve 2.5, so ok. Then at VP_01, turnaround 1 slot, then post-service charge. Can charge up to 3 blocks, each 0.95 kWh. For MISSION_03, we need to depart from VP_01 with enough energy for the flight and reserve. The energy needed for MISSION_03 depends on path. If PATH_05 (energy 0.167248), then required energy = 0.167248 + reserve 2.5 = 2.667248 kWh. If we use PATH_09, required = 1.337981 + 2.5 = 3.837981 kWh. After MISSION_01 and turnaround, SOC before charge is 4.80627 (if we charge after MISSION_01). But we might need to charge to raise SOC for later missions. Battery capacity 22. Since after MISSION_01, SOC=4.80627, we could charge a bit. But we also need to consider MISSION_05 later, which has energy 1.337981 + reserve 2.5 = 3.837981. So we need enough energy. With careful charging, it's possible.\n\nBut we must also consider pad capacity and charger capacity. VP_01 charger capacity 2. No overrides. So we can charge.\n\nNow MISSION_05: origin VP_07, release 23, due 27, travel 1, energy 1.337981. So need to depart from VP_07 after MISSION_03 arrives at VP_07, turnaround, and charge. MISSION_03 arrival at VP_07 depends on depart and travel. If we use PATH_05 with depart 19, arrival 21 at VP_07. Then turnaround 1 slot, post-service charge can happen from slot 22 onward. MISSION_05 release 23, so earliest depart 23. If we depart 23, arrival 24, due 27, ok. Then we need to charge enough for MISSION_05's energy+reserve = 3.837981. Need to ensure SOC at departure >= 3.837981. After MISSION_03 and turnaround, we have SOC after MISSION_03: start SOC after charging from VP_01 minus MISSION_03 energy. So we need to compute.\n\nAlso, UAV_02 chain: MISSION_02 then MISSION_04. MISSION_04 release 8, due 22. MISSION_02 baseline depart 4, arrival 6, turnaround 1 slot, then post-service charge from slot 7 onward. MISSION_04 could depart as early as 8? Actually, MISSION_04 release 8, so depart >=8. Arrival 6, turnaround 1 (slot 7). So charging can happen slot 7? Actually, the normative order: after service flight, reserve check, then destination turnaround (1 slot), then post-service charge. So the UAV arrives at dest at slot = depart + travel_slots. Then turnaround occupies the next slot (the slot after arrival). Then post-service charge can start at the slot after turnaround, i.e., arrival+2. So if MISSION_02 depart 4, travel 2, arrival 6. Turnaround takes slot 7? Wait: if arrival is slot 6, turnaround is 1 slot. Does turnaround occupy slot 6 or slot 7? The problem says \"destination turnaround\" after service flight and reserve check. The slot logic: depart slot is start of travel; travel lasts travel_slots. So if depart 4, travel 2, the UAV is in air during slots 4 and 5? Typically, travel_slots = number of slots the flight occupies. If depart slot 4 and travel 2, the flight occupies slots 4 and 5, arriving at the end of slot 5? Or arrival slot is depart + travel_slots? The baseline for MISSION_02 depart 4, travel_slots=2, they might consider arrival slot = 6? Because they list due_slot 10, so arrival <=10. In the baseline, depart_slot 4, travel slots 2, so arrival might be 6. That suggests that arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots. So if depart 4, travel 2, arrival at slot 6. Then turnaround 1 slot would be slot 7? Or maybe turnaround occurs in slot 6? Typically in these tasks, the UAV is available for next mission after turnaround. Let's examine the baseline decisions for MISSION_03: it has depart_slot 7, and MISSION_01 for UAV_03 depart 4, travel 1, arrival 5. Then UAV_03 has MISSION_03 depart 7. That means after MISSION_01 arrival slot 5, turnaround (1 slot) would complete at slot 6, then charging could happen slot 6? But they depart 7, so they might have charged for 0 blocks. The order: incoming deadhead (none) -> service flight -> reserve check -> turnaround -> post-service charge. So turnaround occupies time after arrival. If arrival at slot 5, then reserve check immediate? The exact slot allocation: I think departure slot is the slot when the UAV leaves. Travel occupies travel_slots slots, so arrival occurs at the end of the last travel slot. Then turnaround takes the next slot. So for MISSION_01, depart 4, travel 1, so occupies slot 4, arrival end of slot 4? That would make arrival slot 4? But the baseline due 6 suggests arrival can be later. Since due is 6, and they depart 4, travel 1, arrival likely slot 5 (depart 4 + 1 = 5). So arrival slot 5. Then turnaround takes slot 6? Then post-service charge could happen from slot 7? But they depart MISSION_03 at slot 7, so they charged 0 blocks, which is fine. So arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots. So for MISSION_02, depart 4, travel 2 -> arrival 6. Turnaround slot 7. Then MISSION_04 could depart at slot 8 if released 8. That matches baseline depart 8. So that works.\n\nNow with the changed circumstances, we need to plan depart slots.\n\nObjective lexicographic: 1) urgent holds, 2) total holds, 3) repair distance, 4) weighted tardiness, 5) delay plus charge wait, 6) energy. Repair distance? Probably sum of distances of paths chosen? Or total distance flown. Weighted tardiness: maybe sum of tardiness (arrival > due) weighted by priority? But all missions routine. Delay plus charge wait: sum of delays (depart - release) plus time spent charging? Not sure. Energy: total energy consumed. So we want to minimize holds, then distance, etc. Since baseline has 0 holds, we want 0 holds if possible. If we can schedule all missions with feasible paths and energy, no holds.\n\nNow check feasibility with constraints.\n\nUAV_01: MISSION_06 release 21, due 28. Path options: PATH_06 (energy 0.12543, travel 2) or PATH_08 (energy 0.147827, travel 2). Both use corridors: PATH_06 uses COR_02, PATH_08 uses COR_04 then COR_07. No closures on those corridors? Check closed_corridor_slots: only COR_10 and COR_11 closures, no COR_02,04,07. Corridor capacities default 2, no overrides. So both paths feasible. Baseline used PATH_06 depart 21, arrival 23, due 28 ok. Initial SOC 7.5, reserve 1.5, enough. No turnaround needed after? Only this mission, so after completion maybe no need. So MISSION_06 can stay as baseline.\n\nNow UAV_02: initial SOC 1.710895. MISSION_02 must use PATH_03 (energy 0.156537) because PATH_07 too energy-intensive. So PATH_03. Corridor capacity override at slot 4 for COR_06 is 1. So if we depart at slot 4, conflict with UAV_03 if it also uses COR_06 at slot 4. So we need to stagger. Options: shift MISSION_02 to depart at 5 or later. Release 4, due 10, travel 2 -> arrival = depart+2 must be <=10. So depart can be 5,6,7,8. If depart 5, arrival 7. Then MISSION_04 release 8, so can depart 8 if turnaround slot 7? Let's see: arrival 7, turnaround slot 8, then MISSION_04 depart 8? But turnaround takes a slot, so depart must be after turnaround. If arrival 7, turnaround slot 8, then post-service charge would be after slot 8? But MISSION_04 release 8, depart slot must be >= release 8. If we depart at 8, that would conflict with turnaround slot? Actually, the order: service flight -> reserve -> turnaround -> post-service charge. So if arrival 7, turnaround occupies slot 8. Then after turnaround (i.e., from slot 9 onwards), we can start next mission's incoming deadhead (none) or directly service flight? The next mission's service flight start is its depart slot. So if turnaround ends at end of slot 8, the UAV is free from slot 9. So depart slot for MISSION_04 must be >= slot 9. Baseline had depart 8, so that must have been with arrival 6, turnaround 7, depart 8. So with arrival 7, turnaround 8, depart must be >=9. But release is 8, so allowing depart 9 is okay, as 9 >= 8. So we could depart 5 for MISSION_02 -> arrival 7, turnaround 8, then depart MISSION_04 at 9. That would work. But due for MISSION_02 is 10, arrival 7 is fine. For MISSION_04, due 22, arrival 9+1=10, fine. So we can shift MISSION_02 to 5, MISSION_04 to 9.\n\nNow UAV_03: MISSION_01 must depart VP_06 to VP_01, travel 1, energy 0.200697. Release 4, due 6. So depart 4 or 5. If we depart 4, uses COR_06 at slot 4, capacity 1 conflict. So we can depart 5, arrival 6, due 6. Good. Then turnaround slot 7? Arrival 6, turnaround slot 7. Then MISSION_03 release 7, depart >=7. But baseline depart 7, but that would depart before turnaround ends? If arrival 6, turnaround 7, UAV free from slot 8. So depart 7 is while in turnaround, not allowed. So we need to depart at least 8. Check baseline: MISSION_01 depart 4, arrival 5, turnaround 6, depart 7. So with depart 5, arrival 6, turnaround 7, depart >=8. So MISSION_03 depart must be >=8. Release is 7, so allowed.\n\nNow MISSION_03 needs a path not closed. PATH_02 closed. PATH_05 closed until 18. PATH_09 open. So feasible paths: PATH_05 if depart >=19, PATH_09 any depart >=8 (since release 7). Also need to consider travel slots and due 24. If PATH_09 travel 14, depart 8 -> arrival 22, due 24 ok. If depart 9 -> arrival 23, due 24 ok. If depart 10 -> arrival 24, due 24 ok. So possible. PATH_05 travel 2, depart 19 -> arrival 21, due 24 ok; depart 20 -> arrival 22, etc. So many options. Energy: PATH_05 energy 0.167248, PATH_09 1.337981. The optimization wants to minimize distance? repair distance: total distance? So PATH_05 distance 1.04 km, PATH_09 distance 8.32 km. So PATH_05 is shorter. Also weighted tardiness and delay plus charge wait. So we'd prefer PATH_05 if feasible and doesn't cause delays.\n\nBut we also have MISSION_05 after: origin VP_07, release 23, due 27, travel 1. So if we use PATH_05 for MISSION_03, arrive VP_07 at slot 21 (if depart 19). Then turnaround slot 22, post-service charge can happen slot 23 onward. MISSION_05 release 23, so earliest depart 23. But after turnaround ends at slot 22, the UAV is free from slot 23. So depart 23 is possible. Arrival 24, due 27 ok. So PATH_05 with depart 19 seems good. Alternatively, if we use PATH_09, arrive earlier, but then MISSION_05 may have to wait until release 23 anyway. So PATH_05 with depart 19 gives no waiting for release. However, we need to ensure energy for MISSION_05. Let's compute.\n\nCharge states for UAV_03: battery 22, reserve 2.5, charge per block 0.95.\n\nInitial SOC: 5.006967.\nMISSION_01: depart 5, energy 0.200697. After service, SOC = 5.006967 - 0.200697 = 4.80627. Reserve check: 4.80627 >= 2.5 ok.\nThen turnaround at VP_01: after arrival 6, turnaround slot 7. Post-service charge can start at slot 8. We can charge up to 3 blocks. For MISSION_03, we depart at 19. So we have slots 8 to 18 for charging (11 slots). Each charge block takes 1 slot? The problem says \"charge_blocks\" attached to a mission occurs at that mission's destination after its service flight and the stated turnaround. It cannot power that mission's incoming deadhead, service flight, or reserve check. Its energy is available only to later missions assigned to the same UAV. So charge blocks are specified per mission. The number of charge blocks is the number of time slots we charge. It occurs at the destination after turnaround. So for MISSION_01, we can specify charge_blocks (0 to 3) that happen after MISSION_01's turnaround. So charging occupies slots at VP_01. Since MISSION_03 is the next mission for UAV_03, that charge will be available before MISSION_03's departure. We can choose charge_blocks for MISSION_01. Then after MISSION_03, we can have charge_blocks for MISSION_03 at VP_07 before MISSION_05.\n\nWe need to make sure we have enough SOC for MISSION_03 departure. Required at departure for MISSION_03: flight energy + reserve. Since we will recharge before departure, the starting SOC for the next mission is: SOC after previous mission's service plus any post-service charge, but limited by battery capacity. SOC after MISSION_01 service = 4.80627. Then we add charge from charge_blocks: each block adds 0.95 kWh. So after c blocks, SOC = min(22, 4.80627 + c*0.95). For MISSION_03 PATH_05, required = 0.167248 + 2.5 = 2.667248. So we have more than enough even with 0 blocks. But we also need to consider MISSION_05 later. Its required = 1.337981 + 2.5 = 3.837981. After MISSION_03 service, SOC will be (SOC at departure) - 0.167248. Then at VP_07, after turnaround, we can charge to raise SOC before MISSION_05. So we can start MISSION_03 with a modest SOC, then charge after to ensure MISSION_05. Battery capacity 22, so we can accumulate energy. So we can set charge_blocks for MISSION_01 to 0 (or some). However, we must also consider charger capacity at VP_01: charger count 2. At the time we charge, we must not exceed charger capacity. Also, pad capacity at VP_01: 2 pads, so only 2 UAVs can be at VP_01 simultaneously (for turnaround/charging). UAV_02 will also be at VP_01 after MISSION_02. So we need to avoid conflicts on pads and chargers. Need to schedule slots.\n\nNow let's design a complete schedule:\n\nUAV_01: MISSION_06 - keep baseline? depart 21, path PATH_06, arrive 23, turnaround? Not needed if no following mission, but maybe we must still do turnaround? The normative order: after service flight, reserve check, destination turnaround, post-service charge. So even if it's the last mission, we must account for turnaround and maybe charge? The baseline has charge_blocks 0, and depart 21, arrival 23, turnaround 24, charge from 25. That's fine, no constraints after. So we can keep.\n\nUAV_02: MISSION_02 depart 5? Need to check initial SOC. If we depart at slot 5, we must be available from slot 5. The UAV is at VP_06 from start, available slot 4. So depart 5 is fine. Use PATH_03, travel 2, arrival 7. Turnaround slot 8, post-service charge from slot 9. Then MISSION_04: depart from VP_01. Baseline had depart 8 but that was with arrival 6, turnaround 7. With depart 5 arrival 7, turnaround 8, we can depart at 9 (release 8). Travel 1 (PATH_10), arrival 10, due 22. Good. Charge_blocks for MISSION_02? We can set to 0 because MISSION_04 energy 0.104358 + reserve 1.5 = 1.604358. SOC after MISSION_02 service: initial SOC 1.710895 - 0.156537 = 1.554358. This is less than required 1.604358? Wait, reserve check requires after service flight, SOC must be >= reserve. That was ok (1.554358 >= 1.5). For MISSION_04, the departure SOC after charging will be the SOC after MISSION_02 service + charge. If we charge 0, then departure SOC = 1.554358, but need to cover MISSION_04 energy + reserve = 0.104358+1.5=1.604358. So 1.554358 is insufficient. So we must charge after MISSION_02 to reach at least 1.604358. So charge_blocks for MISSION_02 > 0. Each block adds 1.2 kWh. With 1 block: SOC = min(16, 1.554358 + 1.2) = 2.754358, sufficient. So charge_blocks = 1. We charge at VP_01 after MISSION_02 turnaround (slot 8? arrival 7, turnaround 8, charge starts at slot 9). Charging takes 1 slot? The charge_blocks value specifies number of blocks (i.e., how many time slots to charge). Each block takes one slot. So if we set charge_blocks = 1, then UAV_02 will be charging at VP_01 during slot 9. Then afterwards, it is ready for MISSION_04. MISSION_04 depart slot 9? But charging occupies slot 9, so it would finish at end of slot 9 and be available for depart at slot 10. So depart must be > charge end sl